  subSections: [
    {
      title: 'Old Encinitas & Leucadia',
      paragraphs: [
        'Downtown bungalows and the funky Leucadia streets west of the tracks — older housing stock, veteran appliances, and owners who would rather fix a twenty-year Maytag than feed a new machine to the landfill. We agree with them more often than not: these platforms were built for exactly this kind of repair, the parts are cheap, and the salt exposure this close to the water is the main thing shortening their lives.'
      ]
    },
    {
      title: 'Cardiff-by-the-Sea (92007)',
      paragraphs: [
        'Cardiff keeps its own ZIP and its own temperament — coastal cottages, tight kitchens, remodels that mixed Bosch and KitchenAid into 1970s footprints. Composer District streets and the campground side both sit close enough to the water that refrigeration coils earn a coastal inspection habit. Yes, 92007 is fully inside our routing — Cardiff calls don\'t get bounced to a "different area."'
      ]
    },
    {
      title: 'Olivenhain',
      paragraphs: [
        'Semi-rural acreage on the east side: horse properties, workshops, long gravel drives, and appliances that work outdoors and in outbuildings. The service profile is its own thing — pellet smokers and built-in grills that get real use, garage freezers holding a season of storage, dust from arena and trail loading condensers the way the inland estates see it. One visit covers the house and the outbuildings; that\'s just how acreage service should work.'
      ]
    },
    {
      title: 'New Encinitas & Village Park',
      paragraphs: [
        'The postwar-to-recent tracts along El Camino Real — family volume territory. Late-model LG and Samsung machines dominate here, and a two-letter display code is usually the opening line of the service call; the corridor\'s hard water does its usual quiet work on dishwashers and ice makers. This is where our truck stocking earns its keep: the high-turnover parts are on board because these streets use them.'
      ]
    }
  ]
};

const acreageNarrative = {
  eyebrow: 'THE OLIVENHAIN DIFFERENCE',
  headline: 'Large Lots Without the Estate Machinery',
  paragraphs: [
    "Fifteen minutes apart, Olivenhain and Rancho Santa Fe both have acreage — and almost nothing else in common as service calls. RSF runs through managers, gates, and property offices. Olivenhain answers its own door, often in work gloves. The properties are owner-run: a main house, a workshop with a second refrigerator, a serious outdoor cooking setup, sometimes a barn fridge keeping medications and produce. Nobody is forwarding estimates to a family office; the owner wants the straight answer, the real price, and everything looked at in one trip.",
    "So that's the shape of an Olivenhain visit: the machine that prompted the call, then the outbuilding units while the truck is parked — because dusty, unconditioned spaces are where refrigeration quietly struggles, and because driving back out next month for the garage freezer serves nobody. Straight talk on repair-versus-replace comes standard; acreage owners can smell an upsell a pasture away, and we'd rather keep the relationship than win one invoice."
  ]
};

